    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` (plus `AcceptanceBase` and a config helper) is the entire suite, and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `customerCode` assignment on `POST /api/owners` following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**What the code does:** on owner creation, `customerCode` is set to `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` where `LAST3` is the upper-cased first three letters of `lastName` and `NNNN` is a zero-padded 4-digit global sequence equal to `(current owner count) + 1` \u2014 computed *before* this owner is saved, so two owners created in sequence get consecutive numbers (e.g. `SMI-0007` then `JON-0008`).\n\n**Changes:**\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `customerCode` field (`@Column(name = \"customer_code\")`) with getter/setter.\n2. **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** (new function class) \u2014 reads the built `@Val Owner`, computes the code from `lastName` + `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, and mutates the owner in place.\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `assignCode` step between `build` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `customerCode` as a read-only property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and it appears in `GET /api/owners/{id}` responses (MapStruct maps it by name automatically).\n5. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres) \u2014 added the `customer_code` column; fixed hsqldb's positional seed inserts in `data.sql` to include the new column.\n\nThe endpoint keeps the DTO-at-the-edges shape: the assignment operates on the entity mid-pipeline, and the value flows out through the existing `RespondWithOwnerCreated` responder and the GET path via the mapper.",
